Replay stock's pre-context block immediately before the IMS context

Counting requests was not enough: the sets nearly matched while the order did
not. Sequence-aligning our AP->CP stream against stock's on identical firmware
(Android B-16035863 vs pmOS r38), per stack, up to the first IMS context:

    ch 0xf5 Telus   116/196 of stock's requests in order    59%
    ch 0xf6 Fizz     61/198                                 31%

The tail of both alignments is the same hole. Stock's last act before asking
for the context is not the profile push but a short block after it, inside the
518 ms (0xf5) / 618 ms (0xf6) between its last SET_DATA_PROFILE and its
SETUP_DATA_CALL:

    0xf5  GET_DATA_REGISTRATION, 0x0d39, 0x0d0b, 0x0d3b, 0x091a, 0x0d0b,
          GET_OPERATOR, QUERY_NETWORK_SELECTION_MODE, GET_DATA_REGISTRATION,
          GET_VOICE_REGISTRATION                              -> SETUP_DATA_CALL(ims)
    0xf6  0x091a, 0x0d3a, 0x0d39, 0x0d0b, 0x0d3c, 0x0d3b, 0x0d3a, 0x0d3c,
          0x0d3c, ..., 0x0d3b, 0x0d17, 0x0d3b, 0x0d39, 0x0d0b, GET_OPERATOR,
          QUERY_NETWORK_SELECTION_MODE, ...                   -> SETUP_DATA_CALL(ims)

Same quintet 0x0d39 -> 0x0d0b -> 0x0d3b -> 0x091a -> 0x0d0b on both, same
four-query tail. We send every one of those ids -- at radio-on, twenty seconds
early -- so the last thing the CP saw before our request was polling churn.

So the table gains a third phase. The boundary is taken from the capture (the
last SET_DATA_PROFILE before the IMS context) rather than a span chosen by
hand, and the rows are replayed from the IMS sequence in a new STEP_PREAMBLE
between STEP_PROFILES and STEP_PDN -- which puts profiles, then the block, then
the context, in stock's own shape. Every row is advisory: a refused row is
still a row the CP saw, so nothing here can fail the context.

The row builder moves from the modem into the client, because two callers need
it now and the IMS object only ever holds a client. Only the MCCMNC
substitution needs the SIM, so the modem passes that string in and keeps
nothing else.

The table also widens by five ids stock sends before its context that it never
covered -- 0x0106, 0x0107, 0x0605, 0x0903, 0x0c14 -- of which 0x0106/0x0107
also close a content divergence, our cell-broadcast range list carrying one
range where stock's carries seven. 0x0605 and 0x0c14 appear on ch 0xf6 only and
so cannot land in a table generated from 0xf5; that is the single-stack
limitation the header already documents. Still excluded on purpose:
GET_CALL_LIST, GET_SMSC_ADDRESS, SETUP_DATA_CALL and the 0x020e/0x020f/0x0247
SIM and ISIM reads -- the plugin drives those subsystems itself.

The retry-limit test's fake peer answers a fixed set of ids and asserts on
anything else, so it gains the preamble's ids and the two re-asserted settings
as plain successes -- they are advisory in the plugin and that test is about
the retry ladder. Without them the default arm trips and the suite bails before
its last ten tests even run.

Two other candidates from the same alignment were measured and dropped rather
than shipped. "We send SET_INDICATION_FILTER and SET_SCREEN_STATE twice where
stock sends them once" was an alignment artifact: stock sends each three times
per stack and we send two, so we under-send by one. "We ask for the context
before our own configuration block finishes" is false on the stack it was
claimed for: our ch 0xf6 block runs 3.5-4.1 s and the context goes out at
5.0 s, and stock has those same ids after its context too.

--- a/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-boot-parity.h
+++ b/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-boot-parity.h
@@ -40,6 +40,18 @@
     MM_SIT_PARITY_PHASE_BOOT,
     /* After it, while the CP is searching and before anything wants a PDN. */
     MM_SIT_PARITY_PHASE_RADIO,
+    /*
+     * The short block stock runs between its last SET_DATA_PROFILE and its
+     * IMS SETUP_DATA_CALL -- 518 ms of it on ch 0xf5, 618 ms on 0xf6. Replayed
+     * immediately before our own IMS context rather than at boot.
+     *
+     * Sequence-aligning our AP->CP stream against stock's on identical
+     * firmware put both stacks at the same place: the ids are ones we already
+     * send, but we send them at radio-on and stock sends them here, so the
+     * last thing the CP sees before our request is 20 s of polling where
+     * stock's is this block.
+     */
+    MM_SIT_PARITY_PHASE_PRE_PDN,
 } MMSitParityPhase;
 
 typedef enum {
@@ -74,42 +86,45 @@
